The lack of memory safety in C and C++ makes them vulnerable to security-critical memory violations. Hardware-assisted memory tagging offers a promising solution to enable memory safety by associating metadata with memory objects to detect violations with low runtime overhead. However, current implementations can not detect intra-object overflows, where memory violations occur within the boundaries of a single object, e.g., between struct fields or class elements. Recent attacks have shown that such undetected vulnerabilities can be used to circumvent advanced security mechanisms. We propose Memory Tagging with Intra-Object detection (MTIO), a unified hardware-assisted memory tagging approach that combines low-overhead object-level protection with fine-grained intra-object detection. Our RISC-V prototype of MTIO demonstrates that this scheme extends coverage to critical intra-object errors while preserving the performance benefits of hardware-assisted memory tagging, offering a practical and effective solution for enhancing memory safety in C/C++ applications.